{
  "name": "vector_light_indoor_cat",
  "saveFile": "minimal_colony.rws",
  "description": "Two bugs reported from play, in one frame. A sealed roofed room at NOON, one torch, and two casters at the same distance on opposite sides of it: a colonist west, a cat east. BUG ONE, INDOORS IN DAYLIGHT. Lamp shadows worked at night and did nothing indoors during the day. VectorLightMath.DaylightScale scaled them by SkyManager.CurSkyGlow, which is MAP-WIDE -- there is no per-cell version -- so at noon it returned 0 for every pawn on the map, including one in a sealed windowless room where no daylight arrives at all and the torch is the only light there is. The gate now asks whether the caster's own cell is roofed, which is vanilla's own predicate on the other side of the same decision: Graphic_Shadow refuses to draw a SUN shadow on a roofed cell, so the two rules partition the map with no gap and no overlap. The beam lane had always asked the roof grid -- VectorLightOverlay.StrengthFor's comment says 'keying on the global value would put every indoor lamp out at noon' -- so this is the two lanes finally sharing one question rather than a new idea. sky_glow is pinned at the top because the whole scenario is meaningless if the run is not actually in daylight. BUG TWO, THE ENORMOUS CAT. VectorLightPawnShadows.ShadowDataOf read race.specialShadowData and then the ThingDef's graphicData.shadowData. Animals declare theirs in NEITHER: a Cat puts it on the PawnKindDef's adult life stage bodyGraphicData, volume (0.25, 0.3, 0.25). So every animal fell through to the human-shaped fallback and was drawn 0.8 cells tall at a 0.3 half-width against a real 0.3 and 0.125 -- a shadow as long as a colonist's and TWICE AS WIDE, under a sprite a third the size. It now reads the resolved BodyGraphic, which is vanilla's other source, and therefore also gets the life stage the animal is actually in. The DESIGN.md claim that 'animals were unaffected' by the same bug's first half was simply wrong and is corrected. THE TWO PAWNS ARE EQUIDISTANT FROM THE LAMP ON PURPOSE so their shadows differ only by species; before the cat fix the two were the same size, which is the thing the capture shows and the two animal probes assert as numbers. The clock is PAUSED so the sun cannot drift out of the daylight the first pin requires, and every cloud lane is held off because the sheet drifts on the tick counter. SetRoof runs after both spawns because SpawnPawn's clear strips roof from each spawn cell, and a hole over either caster would hand it a vanilla sun shadow and defeat the point. A THIRD PAWN, a squirrel, sits north of the torch and MUST CAST NOTHING: it is one of five vanilla animals (Cobra, Duck, Raccoon, Rat, Squirrel) that declare no shadowData at any life stage, so vanilla draws them no shadow ever -- and every one of them was being drawn a full human-sized one. vector_light_pawn_casters reads 2 with three pawns in the room, which is that assertion as a number rather than an absence nobody checked. The two caster probes name their subject BY KIND rather than picking the lowest thing ID in view, so adding or reordering animals here cannot silently repoint them at the squirrel -- which has no shadow data and would report the very fallback numbers this file exists to prove are gone.",
